Aerie-Purpose: Turn automatic picture-in-picture on by default: a playing video enters picture-in-picture when the user switches to another tab, on any site that the user has not blocked (issue #263).
Aerie-Upstream-Area: components/content_settings/core/browser/content_settings_registry.cc (AUTO_PICTURE_IN_PICTURE default), third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5 (BrowserInitiatedAutomaticPictureInPicture)
Aerie-Kind: hook

Chromium already has the whole machinery: AutoPictureInPictureTabHelper watches the tab strip and, when
the tab stops being the active tab while its media plays, asks the media session to enter
picture-in-picture. Two defaults kept it from reaching most videos:

- The content setting AUTO_PICTURE_IN_PICTURE starts at "ask", which also needs high media engagement
  and then shows an allow or block prompt in the picture-in-picture window. It starts at "allow" now,
  so the opt-out rule holds (every protection and convenience on, switchable off). The setting is the
  one that Settings > Privacy > Site settings > Automatic picture-in-picture already shows, with its
  default switch and its list of sites that are blocked or allowed, so a per-site exception is a row in
  that list and the page info of a site has the same toggle.
- A page enters picture-in-picture on its own only when it handles the "enterpictureinpicture" media
  session action. The feature BrowserInitiatedAutomaticPictureInPicture lets the browser do it for a
  page with one playing video and no handler. It is on by default now (the Blink feature follows it).

Neither change lets a page start picture-in-picture. The trigger is still the tab helper's: the tab
stops being active (the user switches tab or Workspace; today the sidebar and Ctrl+Tab do it through
the tab strip), the page is https or file, the media is audible and plays on a top-level frame, and a
site on "block", or a tab that captures the camera or microphone, is left alone. A page cannot move the
active tab by itself.

